# Pantry Scanner udev rule
#
# This rule creates a stable symlink at /dev/pantry-scanner and grants read
# access to the "pantry" group for the barcode scanner's evdev device.
#
# Why not add the service user to the "input" group?
# ---------------------------------------------------
# Adding the service user to the "input" group would grant read access to
# EVERY input device on the box - all keyboards, all mice, all touchpads.
# This turns Pantry into a keylogger for any keyboard ever attached.
#
# The approach here scopes access to exactly one device. If you unplug your
# scanner and plug in a different one (even another scanner), it won't be
# readable by Pantry until you add its identifier here.
#
# The "pantry" group must exist and the container process must be added to it.
# The distroless image runs as uid 65532; add that user to the "pantry" group.
#
# USB scanners:
#   Get vendor/product ID from `lsusb | grep -i scanner`
#   Example: Bus 001 Device 005: ID XXXX:YYYY Symbol Technologies, Inc. Scanner
#   Rule: SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{idVendor}=="XXXX", ATTRS{idProduct}=="YYYY", ...
#
# Bluetooth scanners:
#   Find the device name from `/proc/bus/input/devices` or `udevadm info`
#   Rule: SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{name}=="*Scanner*", ...
#
# For either type, replace the placeholder values below with your scanner's
# actual identifiers.

# USB scanner example (uncomment and edit):
# SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{idVendor}=="XXXX", ATTRS{idProduct}=="YYYY", \
#   KERNEL=="event*", SYMLINK+="pantry-scanner", GROUP="pantry", MODE="0640"

# Bluetooth scanner example (uncomment and edit - replace *Scanner* with actual name):
# SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{name}=="*Scanner*", \
#   KERNEL=="event*", SYMLINK+="pantry-scanner", GROUP="pantry", MODE="0640"
